  "main_findings": [
    "Across 39 TRAIN pairs, no arm achieved any strict-success pair; all four aggregate strict-success rates were 0%.",
    "V1 and V2 recovered compilation only on CWE-116, producing 7/39 final compiled pairs each.",
    "V3 recovered compilation transiently on CWE-116 but regressed by repair4, yielding 0/39 final compiled pairs and 7/39 best compiled pairs.",
    "V0 never recovered compilation on any of the four C-level CWE blocks.",
    "CWE-284, CWE-434, and CWE-639 remained completely uncompilable under all four approaches through repair4.",
    "Additional library reasoning and combined library-plus-skill assistance substantially increased token cost without improving strict success.",
    "CWE-639 was the only shared initial in this four-CWE experiment that actually consumed bounded vulnerable-source exploration."
  ],
  "scientific_interpretation": "The four approaches do not produce a measurable improvement in strict success rate on this 39-pair C-level TRAIN experiment because strict success remains zero for every arm. However, the interventions do affect compilation recovery in a CWE-dependent manner: library-aware reasoning and automatic skill retrieval recover compilation for CWE-116, while the same interventions fail for CWE-284, CWE-434, and CWE-639. This indicates that compilation assistance is not universally effective and that cross-CWE heterogeneity is a central result. The added assistance also incurs substantial computational/token cost, particularly for V1 and V3, without translating into vulnerability detection/discrimination success."
}
